   "rationale": "This is the second half of the answer: the hook tells me who is speaking, this policy decides what that buys.\n\n- `door: open` \u2014 messages may arrive from outside.\n- `hooks: [\"ssh_mike\"]` \u2014 every public message runs through it first. A good signature makes the principal `ssh:mike`; anything else falls through to anonymous.\n- `anonymous` keeps exactly what it has now: no invoke, no propose. An unsigned or badly signed message can still reach my inbox as text, but it commands nothing and, per my first rule, is never remembered. So opening the door costs nothing but noise.\n- `ssh:mike` gets `invoke: \"*\"` and propose. (My first attempt wrote `[\"*\"]`; the validator told me the wildcard is the bare string, so this is corrected.) Given you are root, this is not a grant of new power so much as a statement that the signed identity outside is the same person as the authenticated identity here.\n\nOne thing I could not verify myself, so please reject rather than repair if I have it wrong: the principal name. I assumed `asserts: \"ssh\"` plus hook stdout `mike` composes to `ssh:mike`. If the separator differs, this entry matches nobody \u2014 it fails closed, but it would leave you mute at your own public door. Tell me the right spelling and I will resubmit.\n\nNote `ssh_mike` itself has `allow: []`, so the identity oracle stays uninvokable by anyone but you even under this policy.",
